  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the July 2022 election of the Malaysian Trade Union Congress (MTUC) President, MTUC strengthened its institutional capacity in grappling with labour market transformation and in enhancing services to workers in sectors hit hard by the COVID-19 pandemic. In promoting inclusion and gender equity, MTUC introduced 2 new committees: Women&apos;s Committee and Youth Committee (Chairs of these committees are members of the MTUC board). This innovation allowed MTUC to extend beyond its usual boundaries in terms of representation and expand membership. Moreover, MTUC leadership agreed to revise its constitution and extend protection to workers in emerging sectors such as platform economy workers. 

The ILO and MTUC have been cooperating since 2011 in running Migrant Worker Resource Centres (MRCs) in Kuala Lumpur / Selangor, Penang and Johor. For MRC Central, in terms of case management, a total of 1,769 migrant workers were assisted,156 of them were women. A total of 5 trainings were conducted, with 43 men and 123 women participating. As for organizing, 909 workers were successfully organized into a union. For MRC Northern, a total of 284 migrant workers were assisted, with only 15 of them being women. In terms of training, 4 training sessions were conducted, with 107 men and 1 woman participating.  For MRC Southern, a total of 317 migrant workers were assisted, 290 of whom were women. In terms of training, 6 training sessions were conducted, with 99 woman participating. As for organizing, 44 migrant workers organized into a union. 

The MTUC provided needed legal services to migrant workers in all of the MRCs. 

In July 2023, MTUC leadership agreed to push for the ratification of C87 as it is in the best interest of all workers and the nation. MTUC leadership and members of the Working Committee organized a number of dialogues and as a result, strategy and action plan was formulated in supporting the campaign for ratification of C87.
 The ILO organized a series of consultations with MTUC leaders in tandem with capacity building initiatives, especially in developing innovative strategies in expanding membership and extending representation, by amending its constitution. As a result, two new Committees were set up in this biennium where memberships were extended to women and youth. 

The ILO&apos;s technical advisory services helped with the formulation of a strategy and action plan in campaigning of the ratification of C87.

The ILO organized learning session(s) on C87 as a FPRW and provided MTUC leadership of the need, advantages and importance of this C87 and reasons for ratification.</narrative>
   </description>
